NNAAMMEE
     ppoorrttcchheecckk — validate a port before submitting

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppoorrttcchheecckk [--ddNNPP] [--pp _p_o_r_t_s_d_i_r] [--xx _p_a_t_t_e_r_n]

     ppoorrttcchheecckk --AA [--ddPP] [--pp _p_o_r_t_s_d_i_r] [--xx _p_a_t_t_e_r_n] [_s_u_b_d_i_r _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ppoorrttcchheecckk is used to validate the OpenBSD port or port hierarchy in cur‐
     rent directory.  It should be used before submitting ports for review to
     avoid making common mistakes.  ppoorrttcchheecckk verifies that directory and file
     structure for a port is in place and that no bogus files exist.

     When it's done, ppoorrttcchheecckk will print detected value of port's PKGPATH to
     standard output, unless it fails in detection.  In the latter case, the
     --pp option should be provided.  All other (error) messages from ppoorrttcchheecckk
     end up on standard error output.

     By default, ppoorrttcchheecckk automatically picks up nearest parent directory
     named “ports”, with an optional “mystuff” or “openbsd-wip” subdirectory
     component, as the ports root directory.  For example: if the port being
     imported is located in _/_h_o_m_e_/_j_o_e_/_c_v_s_/_p_o_r_t_s_/_o_p_e_n_b_s_d_-_w_i_p_/_d_e_v_e_l_/_p_5_-_F_o_o, then
     the root ports directory will be detected as being
     _/_h_o_m_e_/_j_o_e_/_c_v_s_/_p_o_r_t_s_/_o_p_e_n_b_s_d_-_w_i_p.  To override this behaviour, see the --pp
     option.

     The following options are available:

     --AA      Intended for running ppoorrttcchheecckk on the whole ports tree, i.e., the
             one lying in PORTSDIR.  This option adds several ignore patterns
             (see --xx option description) and disables some other checks (e.g.,
             for missing distinfo).  PKGPATH determining and printing won't be
             done.  Implicit change of working directory to the ports tree
             root is done before starting any checks.  Also, in this mode one
             or more _s_u_b_d_i_r arguments could be specified, to narrow the check
             only for given subdirectories of ports tree root.

     --dd      Show debugging information such as calling of check routines.

     --PP      Disable expensive checks that use “print-plist-with-depends” tar‐
             get, e.g., proper usage of gtk-update-icon-cache(1),
             update-desktop-database(1) and update-mime-database(1).

     --pp _p_o_r_t_s_d_i_r
             Forces the given directory to be treated as ports root directory.
             Cancels autodetection of the root ports directory made by de‐
             fault.  This option is useful, e.g., when you have a temporary
             ports tree in a non-standard location.

     --NN      Intended to be used when working on new ports.  Enables the
             checks like the presence of REVISION markers and non-0.0
             SHARED_LIBS.  It also enables checks for the presence of CVS di‐
             rectories that could be left by mistake when creating a new port
             based on another one.

     --xx      Excludes files and subdirectories matching given shell globbing
             pattern from any checks.  Note that matching is done against rel‐
             ative path, and not against absolute path or base name either.
             I.e., to exclude the “x11/kde4/libs/logs” from checks, you must
             pass the whole line as argument, not just “logs”.  Multiple -x
             options may be specified.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To validate a new port you've just prepared, go to port's directory and
     run:

           $ portcheck -N

     If you were working on updating of an existing port in CVS tree:

           $ portcheck

     To run a global check of the whole “devel” category in ports tree, use
     the --AA option instead:

           $ portcheck -Ap /usr/ports devel

